Release polish: replace modal_bottom_sheet, unclip the settings cards, hide the
mic button.

Three unrelated defects found while getting keklist to a state worth showing.
Two of them are app choices that a phone forgives and this console does not.

1. modal_bottom_sheet does not work here, in three ways at once.

keklist opens fourteen sheets through `modal_bottom_sheet: ^3.0.0-pre`, via
`showBarModalBottomSheet` and `showCupertinoModalBottomSheet`. On hardware all
three of its failures show up together: the sheet paints as a black rectangle,
taps inside it never reach the content, and no gesture closes it, so the sources
sheet and the emoji picker were both dead ends. Flutter's own
`showModalBottomSheet` is fine in the same build -- the paywall and the password
prompt use it and always worked -- so this is the package, not sheets.

The package positions its sheet with a layout delegate driven by the route
animation and puts a drag-to-dismiss `Listener` above the content. Both exist to
give a phone a pull-down gesture. Neither is wanted here: the Vita has no bezel
worth pulling from, and on a 960x544 landscape screen a sheet covering the
bottom half leaves 272 px for content.

So `lib/presentation/core/widgets/vita_sheet.dart` reimplements both entry
points as a pushed route with an app bar and a close button. The names and the
`context` / `builder` signature match the package, so all fourteen call sites
keep their shape and thirteen of them change by one import line. The fourteenth
is `_showWhatsNew`, which renders a `WebPageScreen` that already brings its own
`Scaffold` and close button, so it pushes directly instead of stacking a second
app bar.

The `Scaffold` in the shim is load-bearing rather than decoration. These bodies
were written to sit inside a container the package supplied: `MindPickerScreen`
returns a bare `Column`, so on a route of its own it has nothing painting
behind it.

The dependency stays in pubspec.yaml, unused. Nothing in lib/ imports it.

2. Settings cards flickered while the list scrolled.

`SettingsSectionCard` rounded its corners with a `ClipRRect`, which at the
default `Clip.antiAlias` is an offscreen layer per card per frame. `Material`
already knows how to fill a rounded shape and does it with one `drawPath` and
no layer, so the radius now comes from the shape and the clip is gone. Four
frames sampled mid-scroll hold 74 to 81 percent card fill with no collapse,
against 60 FPS.

The cost is that a tile's ink splash is no longer clipped at the card's corners.

3. The mic button was live and could not record.

`record: ^7.0.0` has no Vita implementation and the port has no microphone
support to write one against, so the plugin is never registered. The button in
the comment bar still appeared, still opened the recorder sheet, and the sheet
then failed inside `record`.

`AudioCapture` asks once at startup and the button is gated on the answer. It
defaults to no: an unregistered channel raises `MissingPluginException` and is
caught, but a channel with nothing on the other end can also never reply, and
starting from false means that hides the button rather than hanging the screen
that asked. RevenueCat's own `MissingPluginException` in the log confirms this
port takes the first path.

Verified on hardware at 192.168.0.32, GL backend, 60 FPS: sources and the
actions menu open as pages and close from the app bar, an emoji tapped in the
picker selects and pops back to the creator, settings scrolls without dropping
a card background, and the comment bar has no mic button.

diff --git a/lib/presentation/core/platform/audio_capture.dart b/lib/presentation/core/platform/audio_capture.dart
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6bb1135
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/presentation/core/platform/audio_capture.dart
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+import 'package:record/record.dart';
+
+/// Whether this build can capture audio, asked once and remembered.
+///
+/// `record: ^7.0.0` ships implementations for Android, iOS, macOS, Windows,
+/// Linux and web. There is no Vita one, and the port has no microphone support
+/// to write one against, so the plugin is simply not registered and the first
+/// method call comes back unimplemented.
+///
+/// Without this the mic button in the comment bar looks live: tapping it opens
+/// the recorder sheet, which then fails inside `record` and leaves the user
+/// with a sheet that cannot record. Hiding the button says the same thing
+/// honestly and costs one call at startup.
+///
+/// The default is "no". An unregistered channel raises `MissingPluginException`
+/// and is caught below, but a platform channel with nothing on the other end
+/// can also never reply, in which case the await here never returns. Starting
+/// from `false` and only ever flipping to `true` on a real answer means a hang
+/// hides the button rather than hanging the screen that asked.
+abstract final class AudioCapture {
+  static bool _supported = false;
+  static bool _probed = false;
+
+  /// Cached answer. False until [probe] has come back positive.
+  static bool get isSupported => _supported;
+
+  static Future<bool> probe() async {
+    if (_probed) {
+      return _supported;
+    }
+    _probed = true;
+    try {
+      final AudioRecorder recorder = AudioRecorder();
+      await recorder.hasPermission();
+      await recorder.dispose();
+      _supported = true;
+    } catch (_) {
+      _supported = false;
+    }
+    return _supported;
+  }
+}
